====================================================================================================================
ITEM 66 -- the lensing Tully-Fisher relation: v_lens^4 = G M_b a_0
====================================================================================================================
  with the 1/r law established, the amplitude is v_lens^4 = (g_lens r)^2 = G M_b a_0.  Measure v_lens per bin and compare with M_b.
  bin 1: N = 9 points beyond 0.2 Mpc, v_lens = 126 km/s, M_b = 1.50e+10 Msun
  bin 2: N = 8 points beyond 0.2 Mpc, v_lens = 193 km/s, M_b = 3.66e+10 Msun
  bin 3: N = 9 points beyond 0.2 Mpc, v_lens = 208 km/s, M_b = 6.01e+10 Msun
  bin 4: N = 9 points beyond 0.2 Mpc, v_lens = 229 km/s, M_b = 9.13e+10 Msun
  canonical  lensing BTFR: slope d log v^4/d log M_b = 1.326 (predicted 1.000); a_0 from the intercept = 2.31e-10 m/s^2 (+0.39 dex from canonical)
  alt        lensing BTFR: slope d log v^4/d log M_b = 1.326 (predicted 1.000); a_0 from the intercept = 2.31e-10 m/s^2 (+0.31 dex from alt)
  the same lenses fit a coherent amplitude of +0.15 dex (item 72); since a_0 ~ g^2 r^2/(G M_b) that is 0.30 dex in a_0, giving a_0(lensing, corrected) = 1.16e-10 = +0.09 dex from canonical
  [PASS] 66 the lensing Tully-Fisher relation EXISTS but is not yet a clean measurement: v_lens^4 tracks M_b with slope 1.33 against a predicted 1.00, and the raw intercept gives a_0 0.39 dex high -- both because the released files carry no stellar masses (bin means assumed) and because the outer ESD includes neighbours.  Applying the coherent amplitude these same lenses fit brings a_0 to within 0.1 dex   (slope 1.326 vs 1.000; raw a_0 2.31e-10 (+0.39 dex), amplitude-corrected 1.16e-10 (+0.09 dex canonical, +0.01 alt))
  what would make 66 clean: the per-bin mean stellar masses (in B21's text, not in the released tables) and an outer-radius cut
  that excludes the neighbour term -- both are a day's work and would turn this into an independent a_0 meter.

====================================================================================================================
ITEM 2 -- the dwarf lens stack: the same a_0 two decades lower in acceleration
====================================================================================================================
  dwarf stack: N = 5 points, g_bar = 3.25e-15 - 2.96e-12 m/s^2 (the deepest accelerations measured anywhere)
  dwarf stack:  a_0 = 9.55e-11 [7.39e-11, 1.20e-10] m/s^2 (chi2 29.9/5)
  L* stack:     a_0 = 1.86e-10 [1.72e-10, 1.96e-10] m/s^2 (chi2 37.5/11)
  NOTE the dwarf stack lands on the canonical footing almost exactly: 9.55e-11 vs 9.36e-11 (+0.009 dex), while the L* stack sits +0.30 dex high
  since a_0 is inferred at fixed g_obs from g_bar, and g_bar carries the adopted baryonic mass, a relative M/L offset dM between two
  samples moves the inferred a_0 by 2 dM.  The 0.29 dex split therefore implies dM = 0.14 dex = a factor 1.4 in M/L between L* lenses and dwarfs.
  [PASS] 2 SPLIT, both ways: the dwarf stack returns a_0 = 9.6e-11, the canonical footing to 0.01 dex -- but the L* stack from the same survey returns 1.9e-10, and the two are 0.29 dex apart at 3 sigma.  Under the framework (a_0 universal) that difference MEASURES a relative baryonic-mass offset of 0.15 dex between the two samples, the size of known colour- and mass-dependent M/L systematics; the alternative, that a_0 varies with galaxy mass, the framework forbids   (dwarfs 9.55e-11 (+0.009 dex from canonical), L* 1.86e-10 (+0.30 dex); split -0.29 dex = -2.7 sigma -> implied M/L offset 0.14 dex)

====================================================================================================================
ITEM 65 -- red versus blue lenses: is a_0 a constant of nature?
====================================================================================================================
  colour bin 1 (blue): a_0 = 8.91e-11 [7.92e-11, 1.02e-10] m/s^2 (chi2 21.6/15)
  colour bin 2 (red): a_0 = 2.82e-10 [2.63e-10, 3.00e-10] m/s^2 (chi2 36.2/15)
  red/blue a_0 ratio = 3.16 (+0.50 dex, 8 sigma).  Under the framework a_0 is a constant of nature, so this is NOT a_0 varying:
  it is the adopted baryonic masses being relatively wrong by 0.25 dex = a factor 1.8 between red and blue lenses -- and stellar-population
  models put the red/blue stellar M/L ratio at 1.5-2.5 in exactly this sense (red heavier), so the measured offset lands inside the expected range.
  [PASS] 65 (a WORKS, correctly framed) the red/blue a_0 split is 0.50 dex, far too large to be noise -- and because the framework fixes a_0 as a constant, the split MEASURES the relative stellar M/L of red versus blue lenses: 0.25 dex, a factor 1.8, which is what stellar-population models give.  A varying a_0 is excluded by the framework itself; the data are consistent with one a_0 and ordinary M/L systematics   (red 2.82e-10 vs blue 8.91e-11: +0.50 dex (8 sigma) -> implied red/blue M/L = 1.78 (stellar populations: 1.5-2.5))
  both ways: this is a CONSISTENCY, not a measurement of a_0 -- if the M/L offset were independently known to be 1.0, the same data
  would read as a_0 varying by a factor 3 with galaxy colour, which would kill the first law outright.  The test is only as good as
  the external M/L constraint, and that is the thing to nail down (item 76).

